\newlabel{tab:data}{{1}{3}{Shared labelled-data partitions. The adaptation task has 40 input dimensions. Each separate hidden set has 10,000 unlabelled examples}{table.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{1}{4}{Validation accuracy on matched nested training subsets. Both methods use the same rows at each fraction and the same fixed validation set. Full training sizes are 8,400, 14,000 and 22,400 for K20, K30 and K40. Points and error bars show mean and sample standard deviation over three model seeds on the same split. Quadratic logistic regression is deterministic. Lines guide the eye; the dashed line marks 90\%}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{tab:main}{{2}{5}{Local-test accuracy (\%) after fitting each trainable method on 90\% of the labelled data. Entries are mean $\pm $ sample standard deviation across three seeds on one fixed split. Majority and L-BFGS logistic regression are deterministic, with identical repetitions}{table.2}{}}
\newlabel{tab:adapt}{{3}{5}{K40-Adapt local-test accuracy on 400 examples, reported as mean $\pm $ sample standard deviation across three seeds. Trainable models use 3,600 training and validation rows. Zero-shot is deterministic; its repeated predictions are identical}{table.3}{}}
